  "2026-09-08T05:49:39.774Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check: complete all-status 2658-item title/metadata catalog and source ledger reviewed; current query description length stopping nearest pm-fq80,pm-q0ye,pm-7qjk,pm-doxj,pm-88cy. No individual owner for this experiment was found. Shared scorer/signal/packer baselines are prerequisites, not proof of this distinct mechanism. User requested individual planning records; this draft preserves the open cognitive-architecture decision gate and does not accept the ADR. Estimate is a one-day initial spike budget, not a delivery forecast."
body: "## Proposal and provenance\n\nStable algorithm slug: `description-length-stopping`. Source: pm-4k6b note recorded 2026-07-25T06:21:11.864Z.\n\n(25) Minimum-description-length selection with an information-per-token stopping rule. Entries (4) and (9) price redundancy heuristically through marginal value and granularity choice, but neither has a principled place to stop other than the budget ceiling, which means a small workspace still spends its whole allowance. Price context in bits instead: choose the subset that most reduces the description length of the workspace state the agent must reconstruct, and stop when the marginal bits saved per token fall below a declared threshold rather than when the budget is exhausted. This gives three properties nothing else in the catalog provides: a formal account of redundancy (an item that is derivable from already-selected items costs bits and buys none), a natural under-spend on small and unambiguous workspaces which is precisely what pm-z436 needs at three-item scale, and a directly reportable efficiency number - bits per token - that pm-8pnj and the token-cost corpus gate can regress against. The estimator can be cheap: token-level compressibility of the candidate against the already-packed prefix approximates the ideal well enough to rank, and the exact objective stays declared for evaluation.\n\n\n\n## Delivery boundary\n\nEvaluate this mechanism as a bounded public-SDK experiment before any production adoption. Use the existing scorer and packer contracts; no CLI-only behavior or new command noun. The historical catalog is design input, not evidence that this mechanism was implemented.\n\n## Experiment protocol\n\nFreeze a workspace/history revision and parameter manifest. Compare against the current deterministic SDK baseline using held-out judgments and negative controls. Report nDCG/action success, critical-evidence omission, latency p50/p95, peak memory, tokens per completed task, and rebuild cost separately. Include 3-item/minutes, real-shaped, 100k/1M-item, decades-long, and sparse/dense/imbalanced graph scenarios; smoke subsets may not claim scale acceptance. Bound candidate counts, graph expansions, elapsed time, and output size, with cancellation and completeness receipts. Record exact versus approximate behavior and approximation error; theoretical guarantees require their stated assumptions. Missing or untrusted evidence remains unknown, never inferred proof.\n\nPromotion requires a reproducible quality improvement on the held-out target population, no regression of existing exact invariants or mandatory gates, and a declared compute/token cost justified by that improvement. Reject or defer with the same immutable evidence if those conditions fail. No production default, adaptive learning, or data export is authorized by this planning item."
